**user**:
> It is more correct to have a quote end with:
> -- Author, Document
> or 
> -- Document, Author
> ?
**assistant**:
> More correct/common is:
> 
> > “Quote.”
> > — **Author**, *Document*
> 
> That is, **Author first, then the work/source**.
> 
> Example:
> 
> > “The arc of the moral universe is long, but it bends toward justice.”
> > — **Martin Luther King Jr.**, *“Remaining Awake Through a Great Revolution”*
> 
> Use **Document, Author** only if your emphasis is on the document as an object—e.g., cataloging, legal references, archival labels—or if a specific style guide requires it.
> 
> A few typography notes:
> 
> - Use an **em dash** `—`, not two hyphens `--`, in polished text.
> - Italicize book/report titles: `— Author, *Book Title*`
> - Put article/speech/chapter titles in quotation marks: `— Author, “Essay Title”`
> - If the author is unknown: `— *Document Title*` or `— Unknown, *Document Title*`
> 
> So the default best form is:
> 
> > — Author, *Document*
